Subject: [PATCH] Fold in what the Insurance for a Cause build taught the docs

Everything here cost real time on `anchor` and none of it was written down
anywhere. Rules first, the story that produced each one indented underneath, in
the shape the rest of these files already use.

glaze.md, CSS failure log:
- An animated element wider than about 4096px stops being composited on mobile.
  Two marquee rails at 4,510px and 11,488px animated perfectly in every desktop
  browser and sat dead still on a phone.
- A rotation's apparent movement scales with the element's size. The same 2.6
  degrees that sways a 300px mark is a third of the travel at 180px.
- A color token validated against one ground is not validated.

glaze.md, Next.js failure log:
- Anything the server renders open and the client collapses is a layout shift. A
  mobile menu rendered expanded for the no-JS case then collapsed on hydration
  measured CLS 0.3947, and only on a throttled connection.
- On a pitch host, / is the proposal, not the site, so a logo linking home
  throws the client out of their own demo.
- A dashboard build setting survives deleting the file that created it. A repo
  that began as a static pitch kept serving public/ as a flat directory after it
  became a Next app: green build, and every framework route returning Vercel's
  own NOT_FOUND.

glaze.md, Being straight: do not argue the other guy's case in our own pitch.
A price note on the proposal apologized for a comparison that was already
sourced and linked, in two sentences of the competitor's defense written by us,
unprompted. Kevin deleted it. The rule is a test of who pays for the silence:
if leaving it out costs the client money, time or a bad surprise, say it; if it
costs only our comfort, cut it. A sourced figure needs no apology, and a
comparison that cannot be defended without a caveat is a comparison to change
rather than annotate. proposal.md gets the short version in what stays out,
plus a checklist line that greps for "in fairness", "to be fair",
"admittedly", "of course" and "that said".

glaze.md, the quality bar:
- A guess written as a plain value is worse than a blank. Mark it PLACEHOLDER
  even when the guess feels safe, because the next person cannot tell your
  assumption from a confirmed fact.

launch.md: favicon lines in the done list, a 404 line, a note not to put a 404
route in the auditor's list, and a note to measure CLS on a throttled connection
rather than only a throttled CPU.

brand.md: a client favicons section. Two cuts, full bleed with no corner radius,
and three sizes inside the .ico.

link-cards.md: one host, three documents, two icons. Glazed's documents carry
Glazed's mark and the client's site carries theirs, which is the opposite of
what this section said in its first draft. The donut sticking in the tab over a
client's demo is a real failure with a different cause: a document that declares
no icon and falls back to /favicon.ico at the origin root. Declare on both sides
and nothing falls back. Plus the SVG-then-.ico order for Safari, absolute paths
because / and /logo are rewrites, and a checklist line.

proposal.md: the numbered steps glaze and the price melts, both lifted from
glazedweb verbatim, drivers and thresholds included. With the trap on each. On
the glaze: copy only the disc and it looks glazed sitting still and never
glazes, because the two pieces that make it work are the two with no visible
effect on their own. On the price: the anchor is a published figure with a link
under it, and `data-to` holds the real price so nobody with scripts blocked ever
reads a number we are not charging.

intake.md: "what town are they actually in, and which county", asked even when
you think you know. The story now carries both misses, because the second one is
the useful half: it cost one edit and a regenerated image, against a first
correction that touched titles, hero, structured data, a card and four guides.
A fact you got wrong once belongs in exactly one place.

